850: point is finite and hence different from the zero section.  At the
851: \(IV^*\) fibre take \(t=z^3\), \(x=z^4X\), \(y=z^6Y\).
852: Then
853: 
854: \[
855:  P'_*=(0,\sqrt2(z^3-1))\longrightarrow(0,-\sqrt2)
856:  \quad\hbox{on }Y^2=X^3+2.
857: \]
858: 
859: At the \(III\) fibre take \(t=1+z^4\), \(x=z^2X\),
860: \(y=z^3Y\).  This gives
861: 
862: \[
863:  P'_*=(0,\sqrt2\,z(1+z^4)^2)\longrightarrow(0,0)
864:  \quad\hbox{on }Y^2=X^3-3X.
865: \]
866: 
867: Both limiting points are finite smooth points of the good-reduction
868: elliptic curves and are different from the point at infinity.
869: At the nodal fibre put \(q=1/t\), \(X=q^2x\), \(Y=q^3y\).
870: The section is \((0,\sqrt2(1-q))\), with limiting point
871: \((0,\sqrt2)\) on \(Y^2=(X-1)^2(X+2)\).  It is smooth and
872: different from both the node \((1,0)\) and the zero section.
873: The same statements hold for \(-P_*\); fibre inversion gives
874: the exact comparison between these two choices.  Pullback under
875: the actual regular-binding cover preserves these explicit
876: sections and their nonidentity specializations.  Every smooth
877: elliptic fibre therefore carries a nontrivial degree-zero line,
878: whose zeroth and first cohomology vanish.  On each of the twelve
879: nodal fibres the line has degree zero and a nonidentity
880: multiplicative Picard parameter.  The normalization sequence is
881: a two-term complex
882: \(\mathbf C\xrightarrow{1-\lambda}\mathbf C\), where
883: \(\lambda\ne1\); it is acyclic.  The bundle \(nF\) is pulled
884: back from the base and does not affect any of these restrictions.
885: All source identities and root-chart substitutions are checked
886: exactly in `code/part3_fourfold/direct_section.py` and its recorded
887: outputs.
888: Proper cohomology and base change give the vanishing below.
889: More precisely, Stacks Project Lemma 30.22.1, tag 07VK,
890: applies to this proper flat projective elliptic fibration and
891: its line bundle: on each Noetherian affine base open it provides
892: a perfect complex computing the direct image and commuting with
893: arbitrary derived base change.  Every fibre complex is zero by
894: the preceding calculation.  A bounded complex of finite projective
895: modules with this property is zero: at any local ring, cancel
896: every differential entry that is a unit; the remaining minimal
897: complex has zero differentials after reduction to the residue
898: field, so its zero fibre cohomology forces every remaining rank
899: to be zero.  This proves
900: 
901: \[
902:  R f_*(M+nF)=0.
903: \]
904: 
905: Consequently the Dolbeault index is zero.  Combining the actual
906: symbol factorization and cap comparison gives
907: 
908: \[
909:  A=\operatorname{ind}(D_{\bar X,b})
910:   =\operatorname{ind}\bar\partial_{S,M+nF}=0.
911: \]
912: 
913: The computation uses fibrewise acyclicity and the explicit cap
914: switch; it does not reuse the numerical page characteristic
915: calculation in P3K.7.  The integral cover argument P3F.11 again
916: gives zero for the original parameter return.  **End of proof.**
917: 
918: ## P3K.9. Proved: the rotating normal framing and the individual return integers
919: 
920: For the rotating framing specified below, the one-gamma-turn
921: return has Jin characteristic numbers \((k,\ell)=(-6,-24)\).
922: After removing an extending constant-rotation loop, this framing
923: descends through the sixfold parameter cover.  With the descended
924: framing the original positive return has
925: 
926: \[
927:  k=-1,\qquad \ell=-4.
928: \]
929: 
930: Every other tubular framing differs by the explicitly described
931: changes \((k,\ell)\mapsto(k+n,\ell+4n)\) of P3F.11.  The asserted
932: individual values include the framing; their index is independent
933: of that choice.
934: 
935: **Proof: frames on the two pieces.** Give the graph copy of the page
936: its normal frame consisting of its oriented normal in \(Y\),
937: followed by the two Cartesian parameter directions.  The graph
938: derivative changes this frame only by a triangular shear, whose
939: off-diagonal block contracts linearly to zero.  On the compression
940: use a normal frame of \(W\subset Y\times I\), then transport it
941: by the actual gamma rotation \(R_\theta\).  This normal rank-three
942: bundle is trivial.  Indeed \(W\) is a solid torus minus a ball and
943: has trivial tangent bundle, while \(TY\oplus\mathbf R\) is trivial;
944: the Whitney formula gives \(w_2(\nu W)=0\).  Over a three-complex,
945: an oriented rank-three bundle is classified by \(w_2\), since
946: \(\pi_1(SO(3))=\mathbf Z/2\) and \(\pi_2(SO(3))=0\).
947: At the sphere end, its comparison with the fixed positive-Hopf
948: frame is a map \(S^2\to SO(3)\).  This extends over \(D^3\) because
949: \(\pi_2(SO(3))=0\).  Make this extension at parameter zero and
950: transport it by \(R_\theta\).  The resulting frame extends over
951: the whole marked compression cap \(H_a\).
952: 
953: We compute the transition between these two actual frames.  Use
954: radian angular coordinates in this paragraph.  Let \(\phi\) be
955: the fixed outward-collar phase, namely the Milnor-page phase plus
956: \(\pi\), and let \(d=12\) be its degree along the gamma circle.
957: At the binding its outward page ray has angle
958: \(\alpha=\phi-d\gamma\) in the complex \(q-\epsilon\) plane.
959: The exact graph boundary from P3K.6 is
960: \(\gamma=\theta+g_\epsilon(p)\), where
961: \(p\in T^2\) is the source point and \(g_\epsilon\) is now the
962: radian-valued lift of that graph.  No source-dependent change of
963: the binding phase is made.
964: 
965: After quotienting by the source tangent directions and the tangent
966: to this boundary graph, the four remaining coordinates are the
967: real and imaginary \(q-\epsilon\) directions, the parameter radial
968: direction, and the relative angular direction.  The chosen page
969: collar has positive parameter radial derivative, pointing from the
970: page towards the compression.  We use the outward page collar and
971: the inward compression tangent with this same direction.  In the
972: four indicated coordinates its tangent is
973: \(u=(\cos\alpha,\sin\alpha,\rho,0)\), with \(\rho>0\).
974: The explicit quotient morphism, from this four-space modulo
975: \(\mathbf Ru\) to the reference three-space, is
976: 
977: \[
978:  (v_q,v_r,v_\eta)\longmapsto
979:  \left(v_q-\frac{v_r}{\rho}(\cos\alpha,\sin\alpha),v_\eta\right).
980: \]
981: 
982: The Cartesian parameter axes have radial-relative coordinates
983: \((\cos\theta,-\sin\theta)\) and
984: \((\sin\theta,\cos\theta)\), after a positive scaling of the
985: relative angular coordinate.  Project them by this formula,
986: reverse both parameter axes, and apply the positive reference
987: rescaling \(\operatorname{diag}(\rho,\rho,1)\); normalize the
988: page-normal column by the positive factor \(\rho^{-1}\).
989: The graph normal frame in
990: this reference space, after constant oriented changes of frame and
991: positive rescalings, is
992: 
993: \[
994:  G(\alpha,\theta)=
995:  \begin{pmatrix}
996:  -\sin\alpha&\cos\theta\cos\alpha&\sin\theta\cos\alpha\\
997:  \cos\alpha&\cos\theta\sin\alpha&\sin\theta\sin\alpha\\
998:  0&\sin\theta&-\cos\theta
999:  \end{pmatrix}.
1000: \]
1001: 
1002: Here the first column is the oriented page normal, and the other
1003: two columns are the Cartesian parameter directions projected along
1004: the collar tangent.  The coefficients of the source derivatives
1005: of \(g_\epsilon\) enter only the source tangent directions already
1006: quotiented out; they give triangular shears before this quotient.
1007: The positive radial collar coefficient can be rescaled to one.
1008: During collar rounding the transverse vector remains in the same
1009: open radial half-space.  This space is contractible, so this
1010: rounding contributes no additional three-dimensional clutching
1011: class.  These operations specify a homotopy of the actual normal
1012: transition, not merely an equality of its characteristic numbers.
1013: 
1014: Direct multiplication gives \(G\in SO(3)\) and
1015: \(G(\alpha,\theta)=R_z(\alpha)G(0,\theta)\), where \(R_z\)
1016: rotates the first two reference axes.  The compression-cap frame
1017: in these same coordinates has the form \(h(p)\), independent of
1018: \(\theta\): both the ambient product coordinates and this frame
1019: are transported by \(R_\theta\).  Consequently the full transition
1020: on the marked \(T^3\) is homotopic to
1021: 
1022: \[
1023: \begin{aligned}
1024:  h(p)^{-1}G(\phi-d(\theta+g_\epsilon(p)),\theta)
1025:  &=\bigl[h(p)^{-1}R_z(-d g_\epsilon(p))\bigr]\\
1026:  &\quad\cdot\bigl[R_z(\phi-d\theta)G(0,\theta)\bigr].
1027: \end{aligned}
1028: \]
1029: 
1030: The first factor is pulled back from the source \(T^2\), and the
1031: second from the parameter circle.  The cubic Maurer--Cartan form
1032: of a product of two maps is the sum of their cubic forms plus an
1033: exact form; this identity follows by expanding
1034: \((ab)^{-1}d(ab)=b^{-1}(a^{-1}da)b+b^{-1}db\) and using the
1035: Maurer--Cartan equations.  Each of the two cubic forms here is
1036: zero by dimension.  Its integral over \(T^3\) is therefore zero.
1037: The Pontryagin clutching formula can be obtained directly by using
1038: the interpolating connection \(t g^{-1}dg\) on the collar between
1039: two framed pieces: its Chern--Weil integral is a fixed multiple
1040: of that cubic integral.  We conclude
1041: 
1042: \[
1043:  \langle p_1(\bar\nu),[\bar X]\rangle=0.
1044: \]
1045: 
1046: This calculation allows nontrivial one-dimensional and
1047: two-dimensional frame data.  It proves the required vanishing
1048: without replacing the actual normal bundle by a trivial bundle.
1049: The matrix identities and ensuing exact arithmetic are exported
1050: in `code/part3_fourfold/normal_clutch.py` and its JSON and text outputs.
1051: 
1052: **Proof: the integers and their descent.** P3K.7 now gives, with
1053: its full orientation comparison,
1054: 
1055: \[
1056:  k_{\gamma}=\frac{0+3(-8)}4=-6,
1057:  \qquad \ell_{\gamma}=-24+0=-24.
1058: \]
1059: 
1060: We must compare the rotating framing to framings pulled back from
1061: one original parameter circuit.  The fundamental group of the
1062: normal-frame gauge space is
1063: 
1064: \[
1065:  \pi_1\operatorname{Map}(S^2,SO(3))
1066:  =\pi_1(SO(3))\oplus\pi_3(SO(3))
1067:  =\mathbf Z/2\oplus\mathbf Z.
1068: \]
1069: 
1070: Evaluation at one point, with its constant-map section, gives the
1071: displayed splitting.  Pullback through the positive sixfold circle
1072: cover sends \((\varepsilon,n)\) to \((0,6n)\).  Choose any
1073: framing over the original parameter circle and write \(k_0\) for
1074: its Jin integer.  Its sixfold pullback has integer \(6k_0\).
1075: By the exact framing-to-return morphism P3F.11, the free part of
1076: the difference between this pullback and the rotating framing is
1077: \(-6-6k_0\), a multiple of six.  The remaining possible
1078: \(\mathbf Z/2\) part is represented by a constant-axis rotation
1079: loop, independent of the fixed-sphere point.  It extends over the
1080: fixed \(D^3\), contributes zero to the cubic clutch integral, and
1081: changes neither characteristic number.  Remove that extending
1082: loop.  The resulting rotating framing is the sixfold pullback,
1083: up to homotopy, of a framing over the original circle.  We take
1084: this as the descended rotating-framing convention.
1085: 
1086: The replacement diffeomorphism for the pullback is the sixth
1087: iterate of that original framed replacement.  Jin's integral
1088: mapping-torus homomorphism, in the convention of P3F.11, gives
1089: \(6k=-6\) and \(6\ell=-24\).  This proves the stated
1090: \((k,\ell)=(-1,-4)\), including the exact descent of the
1091: normalization.  **End of proof.**
